<hr />
<h2>Chapter 1: Setting Up GBP for Service-Area Businesses</h2>
<h3>The Service-Area Business vs. Brick-and-Mortar Divide</h3>
<p>A service-area business (SAB) visits customers at their locations rather than receiving walk-in traffic. Plumbers, HVAC technicians, roofers, pest control operators, and mobile detailers all fall into this category. Google treats SABs differently from storefront businesses in three critical ways:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Address visibility:</strong> SABs can (and usually should) hide their physical address from public view.</li>
<li><strong>Ranking radius:</strong> SABs without a visible address almost always rank lower than equivalent storefront businesses, according to 2025 local SEO studies.</li>
<li><strong>Service area configuration:</strong> Instead of a pin on a map, SABs define coverage areas by city, ZIP code, or region.</li>
</ol>
<p>Here is the catch: your hidden verification address — not your listed service areas — largely determines where you rank on Google Maps. If you verify your profile using a home address in the suburbs, you will struggle to rank downtown even if you list the city center as a service area. This is the single most misunderstood factor in SAB optimization.</p>
<h3>Creating or Claiming Your Profile</h3>
<p>If you have not claimed your profile yet, start at <a href="https://business.google.com">business.google.com</a>. Search for your business name. If it appears, claim it. If it does not, create a new profile.</p>
<p>During setup, Google will ask whether you have a location customers can visit. Select <strong>"No"</strong> if you do not have a storefront. This flags your profile as a service-area business and enables the address-hiding option.</p>
<p>You must provide a real physical address for verification — even if you plan to hide it. Use your actual business address, home office, or warehouse. Never use a P.O. box, virtual office, or coworking space address. Google cross-references your address against official records, and suspicious addresses trigger suspensions.</p>
<h3>Business Name Optimization</h3>
<p>Your business name must match your real-world business name exactly. Google's guidelines are clear: do not add keywords, locations, or service descriptors unless they are part of your legally registered name.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>❌ Violation</th>
<th>✅ Correct</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>"Mike's Plumbing — Best Emergency Plumber Dallas"</td>
<td>"Mike's Plumbing"</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>"ABC HVAC &amp; Heating Repair Service Frisco TX"</td>
<td>"ABC HVAC"</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>"Top Roofing Contractor — Free Estimates"</td>
<td>"Top Roofing"</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Keyword stuffing your business name is the fastest way to get suspended in 2025. Google's AI enforcement systems flag these violations automatically. A 2025 study by Reinstate Labs found that business name violations accounted for 34% of all GBP suspensions.</p>
<p>If your legal name includes a geographic identifier (for example, "Dallas Roof Pros LLC"), that is acceptable. If you are adding city names to manipulate rankings, you are risking your entire profile.</p>
<h3>Service-Area Configuration</h3>
<p>After verification, configure your service areas strategically:</p>
<ol>
<li>Go to <strong>Edit profile</strong> → <strong>Location</strong> → <strong>Service areas</strong></li>
<li>Add cities, ZIP codes, or counties you actually serve</li>
<li>Keep your coverage within a reasonable drive time — roughly a 30- to 60-mile radius from your verification address</li>
</ol>
<p>Google allows up to 20 service areas. Do not list random cities across your state to cast a wide net. Overreaching dilutes your relevance signals and confuses Google's understanding of your business. A plumber in Austin who lists Houston, Dallas, and San Antonio as service areas will not rank in any of them.</p>
<p><strong>Pro tip:</strong> List your service areas in order of priority. Google may weight the first few entries more heavily.</p>
<h3>Hiding Your Address</h3>
<p>To hide your address from public view:</p>
<ol>
<li>Go to <strong>Edit profile</strong> → <strong>Location</strong></li>
<li>Click the pencil icon next to your address</li>
<li>Toggle <strong>"Show business address to customers"</strong> to OFF</li>
<li>Save changes</li>
</ol>
<p>Your address remains in Google's system for verification and ranking purposes. Customers will see only your service areas, not your street address. This protects your privacy if you operate from home while still allowing Google to geolocate your listing.</p>
<p><strong>Important:</strong> Do not toggle your address visibility on and off repeatedly. Frequent changes to core profile data trigger Google's spam filters and can lead to re-verification requests or soft suspensions.</p>
<h3>Primary and Secondary Category Selection</h3>
<p>Your primary category is the single most powerful ranking signal in your GBP. According to the 2025 Local Search Ranking Factors survey, primary category selection carries more weight than any other on-profile factor.</p>
<p>Google gives you one primary category and up to nine secondary categories. Only your primary category is visible to customers, but secondary categories help Google understand the full scope of your services.</p>
<h4>Category Selection Framework</h4>
<p>Use the "This business IS a..." test. Your primary category should complete the sentence naturally: "This business IS a [category]." It should not be something you merely offer.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Wrong</th>
<th>Right</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>"This business IS a water heater installation."</td>
<td>"This business IS a plumber."</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>"This business IS a bathroom remodel."</td>
<td>"This business IS a kitchen remodeler."</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
