<p><strong>HVAC nuance:</strong> If air conditioning repair drives 70% or more of your revenue, test "Air Conditioning Repair Service" as your primary category. A 2025 analysis by Anthony Louis Media found that HVAC contractors using query-specific primary categories achieved a 45% top-3 placement rate for those searches, compared to 15% for those using the broader "HVAC Contractor" category. If your heating and cooling work is evenly split, stick with "HVAC Contractor."</p>
<p><strong>Electrician nuance:</strong> Electricians perform best with either zero or three secondary categories. Adding five or more secondary categories correlated with weaker rankings in competitive markets, likely because it diluted Google's understanding of the business focus.</p>
<h4>Category Best Practices</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Audit quarterly:</strong> Search trends shift. A category that worked six months ago may not be optimal today.</li>
<li><strong>Align with your website title tag:</strong> If your homepage title is "Licensed Plumber in Phoenix | Mike's Plumbing," your primary category should be "Plumber."</li>
<li><strong>Test for 60-90 days:</strong> Changing your primary category too frequently triggers re-verification. Pick one, commit to it, and measure results.</li>
<li><strong>Avoid generic categories:</strong> "Contractor" or "Home Services" as a primary category is too broad. You will compete against every type of contractor in your area.</li>
</ul>
