<h2>Chapter 3: The GBP Photo Strategy</h2>
<h3>Why Photos Matter More Than Ever</h3>
<p>Businesses with 100 or more photos on their GBP receive 42% more direction requests than those with fewer than 10, according to 2025 BrightLocal data. For contractors specifically, photos are your portfolio. A homeowner deciding between three roofers will click through every photo on each profile before making a call.</p>
<p>Google's AI can now analyze the content of your photos. It recognizes vehicles, equipment, uniforms, job sites, and before/after transformations. Authentic, location-specific photos send stronger relevance signals than generic stock imagery.</p>
<h3>Photo Types That Convert</h3>
<p>Upload photos across these categories in a 3:2:1 ratio:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Category</th>
<th>Ratio</th>
<th>Examples</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>At work / job sites</strong></td>
<td>3 parts</td>
<td>Team performing services, equipment in action, work in progress</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Before/after transformations</strong></td>
<td>2 parts</td>
<td>Side-by-side project photos, completed renovations, repaired systems</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Team and vehicles</strong></td>
<td>1 part</td>
<td>Uniformed crew, branded trucks, office or warehouse</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>At work photos:</strong> These are your highest-converting images. Show your team on actual job sites, wearing branded uniforms, using professional equipment. A photo of a technician repairing an AC unit on a rooftop in your service area is worth 50 stock photos of a smiling family.</p>
<p><strong>Before/after photos:</strong> These are your most powerful trust-building tool. They prove you can deliver results. Capture the "before" state at the start of every project and the "after" state at completion. Upload them as pairs when possible.</p>
<p><strong>Team and vehicle photos:</strong> Branded vehicles act as mobile billboards and profile credibility signals. A clean, wrapped truck with your logo and phone number tells customers you are a legitimate operation, not a fly-by-night operator.</p>
<h3>Photo SEO: Geotagging, Naming, and Compression</h3>
<p><strong>Geotagging:</strong> Add GPS coordinates to your photos before uploading. While Google strips EXIF metadata from uploaded images, many local SEO practitioners have documented ranking improvements after systematically geotagging photos, particularly for relevance and distance signals.</p>
<p>To geotag photos:<br />
1. Find your business coordinates by right-clicking your pin in Google Maps<br />
2. Use a free tool like GeoTag.World or GeoImgr to embed coordinates<br />
3. Apply consistent coordinates across all photos taken in your primary service area</p>
<p><strong>File naming:</strong> Rename photos before upload. Use descriptive, keyword-rich filenames:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>❌ Bad</th>
<th>✅ Good</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>IMG_1234.jpg</td>
<td>roof-repair-houston-tx-before.jpg</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>photo1.jpg</td>
<td>ac-installation-dallas-team.jpg</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>image_05.jpg</td>
<td>emergency-plumber-fixing-burst-pipe.jpg</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Compression:</strong> Reduce file sizes for fast loading. Use TinyPNG or ImageOptim. Aim for under 200KB per photo without visible quality loss.</p>
<h3>Photo Upload Frequency</h3>
<p>Upload 2-3 new photos every week. Regular photo uploads signal an active, operating business to Google. Bulk uploading 50 photos once a quarter does not have the same impact as a steady drip of fresh content.</p>
<p><strong>Weekly photo workflow:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Monday: Upload 1-2 job site photos from the previous week</li>
<li>Wednesday: Upload 1 before/after pair from a recent project</li>
<li>Friday: Upload 1 team or vehicle photo</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Seasonal refresh:</strong> At the start of each season, audit your photos. Remove outdated images (holiday decorations from last year, photos of employees who no longer work for you) and add seasonally relevant content (spring landscaping, summer AC maintenance, fall gutter cleaning, winter heating tune-ups).</p>
<h3>What Not to Upload</h3>
<ul>
<li>Stock photos (customers recognize them instantly)</li>
<li>Blurry or poorly lit images</li>
<li>Photos with watermarks from other websites</li>
<li>Images containing personal information (license plates, house numbers if the customer has not consented)</li>
<li>Photos taken outside your service area (they confuse Google's geolocation signals)</li>
</ul>
