<h2>Chapter 6: The Q&amp;A Section as Lead Gen</h2>
<h3>The Q&amp;A Landscape in 2025</h3>
<p>Google is phasing out the traditional Q&amp;A feature on Business Profiles. As of late 2025, the Q&amp;A API was discontinued, and public Q&amp;A threads are being replaced by AI-generated answers powered by Gemini. When a user asks a question about your business in Google Maps, Google's AI now generates an instant response by pulling from your profile data, website content, reviews, and photos.</p>
<p>This changes the strategy completely. Instead of seeding static Q&amp;A questions, you must optimize every signal the AI can access.</p>
<h3>Optimizing for AI-Powered Answers</h3>
<p><strong>1. Business description:</strong> Include your services, service areas, and booking process. The AI pulls directly from this field when answering "What services do you offer?" and "Do you serve [city]?"</p>
<p><strong>2. Services section:</strong> List every service with a detailed description. If a user asks "Do you install tankless water heaters?" the AI checks your services catalog first.</p>
<p><strong>3. Website FAQ pages:</strong> Create comprehensive FAQ pages on your website covering:<br />
- Do you offer free estimates/consultations?<br />
- What areas do you serve?<br />
- How quickly can you respond to emergencies?<br />
- What payment and financing options do you offer?<br />
- Do you offer warranties or guarantees?<br />
- Are you licensed and insured?<br />
- What brands do you work with?</p>
<p><strong>4. Google Posts:</strong> Weekly posts with offers and service spotlights feed the AI with current, relevant information about what you are promoting right now.</p>
<p><strong>5. Reviews:</strong> Encourage customers to mention specific services and locations in their reviews. The AI synthesizes review content when answering questions about quality, reliability, and service scope.</p>
<p><strong>6. Photos:</strong> Descriptive photo captions and filenames help the AI understand your capabilities. A photo captioned "Tankless water heater installation in Dallas" trains the AI to associate your business with that service.</p>
<h3>Monitoring and Responding</h3>
<p>While traditional Q&amp;A is being phased out, some profiles still display existing Q&amp;A threads. Monitor these regularly and respond to new questions within a few hours. Even if the feature disappears entirely, the practice of monitoring customer inquiries trains you to address the questions your AI answers should cover.</p>
<h3>Competitor Q&amp;A Monitoring</h3>
<p>Check your competitors' profiles weekly for new questions. The questions customers ask them are the same questions they will ask Google's AI about your business. If a competitor's Q&amp;A shows frequent questions about "same-day service" or "financing options," make sure your profile and website explicitly address those topics.</p>
