<h2>Chapter 8: Advanced GBP Tactics</h2>
<h3>Multi-Location Management</h3>
<p>If you operate multiple service areas with distinct physical locations, you need a structured approach to GBP management.</p>
<p><strong>Account architecture:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Use <strong>Location Groups</strong> in Google Business Profile Manager to organize listings under one master account</li>
<li>Assign tiered permissions: corporate maintains "Owner" status, local managers get "Manager" access</li>
<li>Implement unique store codes for each location to keep data organized during bulk updates</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>The 70/30 content rule:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>70% centralized:</strong> Brand standards, core photography guidelines, response templates, content calendars</li>
<li><strong>30% localized:</strong> Neighborhood mentions in descriptions, actual location photos, local events, store-specific promotions</li>
</ul>
<p>Copy-pasting identical content across listings triggers Google's duplicate content detection. Each profile must have unique elements.</p>
<p><strong>Critical multi-location rules:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Link each profile to a <strong>location-specific landing page</strong>, never the homepage</li>
<li>Maintain unique phone numbers per location (shared numbers weaken local signals)</li>
<li>Use the same primary category across the brand; secondary categories can vary by market</li>
<li>Run monthly duplicate audits — acquisitions and vendor changes often create conflicting listings</li>
</ul>
